{"hash":"88196a51e30d9403","voice":"en-IN-NeerjaExpressiveNeural","rate":"+4%","dur":93.69,"said":"You have climbed onto the temple platform. Look up: the shikhara rises about one hundred and fifty feet, crowned by a kalash of about ten tonnes. Temple tradition says the very first shrine here was built by the Moon himself, out of gold; after it came temples of silver, of wood, and of stone. A thousand years ago, in January 1026, Mahmud of Ghazni raided Somnath, broke the linga and carried away its wealth. Think of the grief of the priests and townsfolk that winter. Yet an inscription suggests the temple was repaired within twelve years, and about a century and a half later the Chaulukya king Kumarapala rebuilt it in fine stone. In the centuries that followed it was attacked again and again, and each time devotees and rulers brought the worship back. In the late eighteenth century Ahilyabai Holkar, the queen of Indore, built a new temple beside the ruins; it still stands nearby. And then came the promise you heard about at the gate. In 1951, India’s first President, Dr Rajendra Prasad, consecrated the Jyotirlinga in this new temple, on the ancient site. Now walk on through the great halls toward the sanctum.\n\nDid you know? Somnath holds three aartis every day, at 7 am, at noon and at 7 pm; outside the monsoon, the ‘Jai Somnath’ light and sound show, in Amitabh Bachchan’s voice, follows in the evening.","segments":[{"key":"text","start":0,"end":1127},{"key":"didYouKnow","start":1143,"end":1324}],"words":[[0.098,0.156,0,3],[0.266,0.168,4,8],[0.447,0.445,9,16],[0.903,0.288,17,21],[1.204,0.108,22,25],[1.324,0.421,26,32],[1.757,0.685,33,41],[3.365,0.24,43,47],[3.618,0.252,48,50],[4.074,0.12,52,55],[4.207,0.409,56,64],[4.627,0.493,65,70],[5.132,0.349,71,76],[5.493,0.264,77,80],[5.769,0.288,81,88],[6.07,0.12,89,92],[6.202,0.361,93,98],[6.574,0.385,99,103],[7.26,0.325,105,112],[7.596,0.144,113,115],[7.752,0.048,116,117],[7.812,0.361,118,124],[8.185,0.144,125,127],[8.341,0.